{"reference":"Ezra 10","book":"Ezra","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Ezra 10 with Ezra 9","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Ezra 10 continues the response begun in Ezra 9: Ezra’s grief and confession lead to a communal process addressing marriages with foreign women, involving priests, Levites, and laymen. The account treats this as communal unfaithfulness and records a decision affecting wives and children, but it does not supply the full legal reasoning, the women’s perspectives, or the eventual outcomes in every case. Its setting is a vulnerable Persian-period temple community; this passage therefore should not be treated as a direct authorization for modern ethnic exclusion, forced divorce, theocratic state power, or coercive religious reform.","verse_refs":["Ezra 9:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ezra_010_9ded86d9c193f53e7e117514"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}